All entrances to the cave known as "Seven Sisters" are at least 100 to 250 meters (328 to 820 feet) in diameter.
Infrared images show that temperature variations between night and day indicate that these are likely caves.
Their temperature during the day is cooler than the surrounding surface temperature, and warmer at night.
According to Glenn Creighton of the United States Geological Survey (USGS), the team of astronomers at Northern Arizona University in Flagstaff, Arizona: "Their thermal behavior is not indicative of the stability of large caves on Earth, where temperatures are often relatively constant, but rather resembles deep pits in the ground."
In France, voting has traditionally been conducted using basic technology: voters stood in individual booths and placed a pre-printed paper ballot, indicating their chosen candidate, into an envelope.
After the voter's identity has been verified by the officials, the voter places the ballot paper into the ballot box and signs their name on the voter list.
France's election law establishes relatively strict rules regarding these practices.
Since 1988, ballot boxes must be transparent so that voters and observers can verify that at the beginning of voting no envelope is present inside and that no envelope other than those of eligible voters is added.
Candidates may appoint representatives to observe each stage of the process. At night, votes are counted by volunteers following specific procedures, under strict supervision.
