When a small group of living organisms (a small community) separates from the main community to which it belongs (for example, when they move to the other side of a mountain range or river, or when they go to a new island from which they cannot easily return), they often find themselves in an environment different from their previous one.
This new environment has different resources and competitors, so a new population, in order to become a strong competitor, will need abilities or adaptations different from those previously required.
The population has not changed in any way, and they still require the previous adaptations to the environment.
Over time, as they gradually adapt their population to the new environment, they become less and less similar to other people.
Eventually, after thousands or even millions of years, the two populations will be so different that they cannot be considered part of the same species.
We call this process speciation, which means solely the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and an important part of evolution.
