The Great Pyramid of Giza is the only surviving wonder of the Seven Wonders.
The Great Pyramid, built by the Egyptians in the third century BCE, is one of several large pyramidal structures constructed in honor of a deceased pharaoh.
Giza Plateau, or the "Giza Pyramid Complex," in the Valley of the Kings in Egypt includes several pyramids (among which the Great Pyramid is the largest) and several small tombs, temples, and the large statue of the Sphinx located in that valley.
The Great Pyramid was built in honor of the pharaoh Khufu, and many smaller pyramids, tombs, and temples were constructed in honor of Khufu's wives and family members.
