Subsistence agriculture is agriculture carried out solely to produce enough food to meet the needs of the farmer and his family.
Subsistence agriculture is a simple system, often organic, that uses locally stored seeds from specific ecosystems along with crop rotation or other relatively simple methods to maximize yield.
Historically, most farmers have earned their livelihood through agriculture, and this still exists in many developing countries.
Subcultures bring together like-minded individuals who feel neglected according to societal standards and help foster a sense of identity development among them.
Subcultures can differ according to the age, ethnicity, social class, place of residence, and/or gender of their members.
Features that distinguish a subculture may be linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographic, or a combination of these features.
Members of a subculture often indicate their membership through distinctive and symbolic use of style, which includes fashion, mannerisms, and deliberate speech patterns.
One of the most common methods used to illustrate the importance of socialization is referring to cases of children who, during their development, failed to learn socialization due to neglect, bad luck, or intentional mistreatment by adults in society.
These types of children are called "wild children." Some of these wild children are imprisoned by people (usually their own parents); in some cases, the abandonment of the child results from parents' denial of the child's severe mental and physical disabilities.
Wild children may have experienced severe abuse or emotional trauma before running away or leaving home.
It is claimed that the rest were raised by animals; it has been said that some lived alone in the wild.
If completely raised by animals other than humans, a feral child exhibits behaviors (within physical limits) almost identical to those of animals, such as fear and indifference toward humans.
Although project-based learning should make learning easier and more interesting, scaffolding goes a step further.
Scaffolding is not a learning method in itself, but rather support provided to individuals who are undergoing a new learning experience, such as using a new computer program or starting a new project.
Scaffolds may be virtual or physical; in other words, a teacher is a type of scaffold, but a paper clip in Microsoft Office is also a scaffold.
Virtual scaffolds are embedded within the embedded software, and their purpose is to question, stimulate, and explain methods that might prove challenging for students to carry out independently.
