Outside the savanna, meeting the amino acid-based nutritional needs from available plant sources proved to be a difficult task for a primate with a digestive system similar to that of humans.
Moreover, failure to do so has serious consequences: depression, malnutrition, and ultimately death.
The most accessible plant sources of protein are found in leaves and legumes, but these are difficult for mammals like us to digest unless they are cooked.
In contrast, animal foods (ants, termites, eggs) are not only easily digestible but also provide high-quality proteins containing all essential amino acids.
Everything has been taken into account, and if our ancestors nearly solved their "protein problem" in the same way that chimpanzees do today in the savanna, we should not be surprised.
Sleep disturbance is the process of becoming consciously aware during normal sleep and then falling back asleep again after a period of time (10-60 minutes).
This can easily be done using a relatively quiet alarm clock that will alert you without fully waking you from sleep.
If you realize that you are turning off your alarm clock while still asleep, you can place it on the other side of the room so that you are forced to get out of bed to turn it off.
Other circadian rhythm-based options include drinking a large amount of fluids (especially water or tea, a specific diuretic) before bedtime, which forces the individual to wake up from sleep to urinate.
