Be resolute in discouraging men and resist without fear (it doesn't improve the situation, whether there are cultural differences or not!).
The modern city of Casablanca was founded by Berber fishermen in the 10th century BCE, and the Phoenicians, Romans, and Merenids used it as a strategic port, calling it Anfa.
The Portuguese destroyed it and then rebuilt it under the name of Casablanca, and they only abandoned it after the earthquake of 1755.
The Sultan of Morocco rebuilt the city of Dar al-Beida, and the Spanish merchants who established their trading posts there named it Casablanca.
Casablanca is one of the cities in Morocco whose shopping locations have the least appeal.
Around the old Medina, you can find places selling traditional Moroccan goods, such as tagines, pottery, leather items, hookahs, and a full range of patterns, but all these items are intended solely for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in the east, near Rwanda.
In 2002, Goma was destroyed by volcanic eruptions from Mount Nyiragongo, which buried most of the city's streets, especially the city center.
Although security in Goma has been established with accountability, any visit outside Goma must be carefully assessed to gain awareness of the ongoing conflict situation in North Kivu province.
This town also serves as a base for climbing Mount Nyiragongo, along which the most accessible routes to the mountain gorillas in Africa are found.
You can use motorcycle taxis to get around the city of Goma. The usual local price for a short ride is around 500 Congolese francs.
The city of "Timbuktu," which has had almost no access, has caused its name to be used as a metaphor for strange, remote places.
Today, Timbuktu is a poor town, although due to its historical significance it has tourist appeal and also has an airport.
In 1990, it was added to the List of World Heritage in Danger due to the threat posed by desertification.
That stop was one of the main stops during Louis Gates' special PBS program titled "Africa's Wonders."
This city is clearly at odds with other cities in this country, as it is predominantly characterized by Arab traits rather than African ones.
Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in northeastern South Africa and extends from the eastern border with Mozambique and from the northern border with Zimbabwe, and its southern boundary is the Crocodile River.
This park covers an area of 19,500 square kilometers and is divided into 14 distinct biogeographical realms, each supporting different wildlife.
One of the main attractions of South Africa, and considered the national flower of South Africa's National Parks (SANParks).
As with all national parks in South Africa, daily conservation and entry to this park also incur a fee.
Buying a game park pass that provides access to selected parks in South Africa or all of South Africa's national parks can be beneficial.
Hong Kong Island has given its name to the territory of Hong Kong, and is a place that many tourists regard as their primary destination for tourism.
The skyline of Hong Kong, formed by its buildings, resembles a bright bar chart clearly visible alongside the waters of Victoria Harbour.
To get the best view of Hong Kong, leave the island and head toward the opposite shore of Kowloon.
The greater part of Hong Kong Island's urban development along the northern coastal line has densely occupied reclaimed and redeveloped land.
This is a place that the British colonists seized for themselves, so if you are looking for evidence of the colonial past of this land, this is a good place to start.
Sundarbans is the largest continuous mangrove forest in the world, stretching 80 kilometers (50 miles) from the coasts of Bangladesh and India inland.
Sundarbans has been designated as a UNESCO World Heritage site. The forested area located within the Indian territory of the Sundarbans National Park is called Sundarbans.
These forests are not only the swamps of Karna—they also contain some of the last remaining remnants of the vast forests that once covered the Sindh and Ganges plains.
The Sundarbans cover an area of approximately 3,850 square kilometers, about one-third of which is composed of water/swamps.
Sundarbans has been a wildlife sanctuary since 1966, and it is estimated that currently 400 royal Bengal tigers and about 30,000 deer have been sighted in this region.
Buses depart throughout the day from the inter-district station (opposite the river), but most of them, especially those bound for the east, Jakarta/Bumthang, depart between 06:30 and 07:30.
Since intercity buses are often full, it is recommended to book tickets a few days in advance.
