Shared taxis are one of the fast and convenient ways to travel to nearby locations such as Paro (Nu 150) and Punakha (Nu 200).
The Oiapoque River Bridge is a cable bridge. This bridge spans the Oiapoque River to connect the cities of Oiapoque in Brazil and Saint-Georges de l'Oyapock in French Guiana.
The height of the two towers is 83 meters, the length is 378 meters, and it has two lanes with a width of 3.50 meters.
The vertical clearance under the tower is 15 meters. Construction was completed in August 2011, and it entered service for passage and inspection in March 2017.
The completion date is September 2017, that is, the expected time for completion of the customs stations.
The Guaraní were the most important indigenous group living in present-day eastern Paraguay, leading a semi-nomadic lifestyle based on hunting and practicing subsistence agriculture.
Chaco was the residence area of other indigenous groups such as the Guaycurú and Payaguá, who sustained themselves through hunting, gathering, and fishing.
In the 16th century, Paraguay, which had previously been known as the "Great Land of the Indians," came into existence as a result of the encounter between Spanish conquistadors and native groups.
The Spanish began the colonial period, which lasted three centuries.
Paraguay has managed to preserve many of its indigenous characteristics and traits since the founding of Asunción in 1537.
Argentina is renowned for having one of the best national teams and the best players in the world.
The biggest race of the year takes place in December at the Chogán fields of Las Canitas.
Tournaments and smaller competitions can also be seen here at other times of the year.
For information about tournament news and ticket purchase locations for the polo tournaments, please visit Asociacion Argentina de Polo.
The official currency of the Falkland Islands is the Falkland Islands pound (FKP), which is pegged at par with the British pound (GBP).
In the only bank in the islands, located in Stanley and along the FIC West shop, currency exchange is available.
The British pound is usually accepted throughout the islands, and Visa and American dollar cards are also generally accepted.
Credit cards may not be accepted in more remote islands, although British and U.S. currency is accepted; please arrange the accepted payment method in advance with the owners.
Foreign currency exchange in the Falkland Islands outside the islands is almost impossible, so exchange your money before leaving the islands.
Since Montevideo is located in the Southern Hemisphere, when it is winter in the Northern Hemisphere, it is summer there, and vice versa.
Montevideo is a subtropical region; during the summer months, temperatures usually rise above 30 degrees Celsius.
Winter can be deceptively cold: temperatures rarely drop below freezing, but wind and humidity combine to make you feel colder than the thermometer indicates.
There is no distinct dry or rainy season: the amount of rainfall remains almost constant throughout the year.
Although many animals in the park are accustomed to seeing people, wild animals are still wild and should not be fed or disturbed.
Keep at least 100 meters away from bears and wolves, and 25 meters away from other wild animals!
American bison, elk, moose, bears, and nearly all large animals, no matter how tame they may seem, can attack.
Every year, dozens of visitors are injured due to not maintaining a proper distance. These are large, wild, and inherently dangerous animals, so do not enter their vicinity.
In addition, please note that odors attract bears and other wild animals, so avoid carrying or cooking fragrant foods and keep your campsite clean.
Apia is the capital city of Samoa. This town is located on the island of Upolu and has a population of slightly less than 40,000.
Apia was established in the 1850s and has been the official capital of Samoa since 1959.
This port is famous for the incident in 1889 when a fleet of seven ships from Germany, the United States, and Britain failed to leave the port.
Besides one British warship, all the ships sank, and nearly 200 Americans and Germans lost their lives.
