Due to Auckland's location between two harbors, there are many beaches. The most popular ones are located in three areas.
The North Shore (in the North Harbour area) of the Pacific Ocean extends from Long Beach to Davenport.
They are almost all sandy beaches where swimming is safe, and most of them have shade from pohutukawa trees.
The Tamaki Drive coastline in Whitemata Harbour is located in the suburbs of the well-known areas of Māngere and Saint Heliers in central Auckland.
These are family beaches, sometimes busy, with good shops along the shoreline. Swimming is safe.
The main local beer is called "Number One," which is not a complex beer, but is pleasant and refreshing. The other local beer is called "Manta."
There are many French wines available for consumption, but wines from New Zealand and Australia may be better preserved.
Drinking local tap water is completely safe, but if you are concerned, you can easily find bottled water.
For Australians, "flat white" is an unfamiliar concept. A short black is the same as an espresso, a cappuccino is served with a generous amount of foam (and not just the crema), and tea is served without milk.
The hot chocolate meets Belgian standards. The fruit juices are expensive but excellent.
Many trips take place throughout the year to coral reefs, and injuries resulting from these factors at coral reefs are rare.
However, heed the advice of officials, follow the signs, and pay close attention to safety warnings.
