The stirrup is a support for the rider's foot, hanging from both sides of the saddle.
Although they provide greater stability for the rider, they may pose safety concerns because the rider's legs could get caught in them.
If a rider falls off a horse and his foot gets caught in the stirrup, the horse will be dragged along the ground if it runs away. To minimize this risk, many precautionary measures can be taken.
First of all, most motorcyclists wear chukka boots for motorcycling, which have a heel and a narrow, completely pointed toe.
In the next stage, some saddles, particularly English saddles, have safety girths that allow the stirrup leather to detach from the saddle if the rider falls.
